ROLE
You are a senior Android engineer and accessibility specialist. Build a production-ready Android app named "Accessible Camera and Gallery". Keep accessibility as the highest priority. Use a very simple interface. Do not use emojis anywhere in the app, UI strings, logs, documentation, or code.

OBJECTIVE
Create a complete Android application that provides camera, video recording, gallery browsing, editing, and settings with strong voice guidance and full screen-reader accessibility. Support every camera and gallery feature that the device hardware and Android platform can support. Where a feature is unsupported, hide it or disable it and provide a clear spoken and visible reason. Do not simulate unsupported features.

TECHNICAL CONSTRAINTS
- Platform: Android, Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3.
- Minimum SDK: 26. Target and compile against the latest stable Android SDK.
- Architecture: MVVM with clean separation of UI, domain, and data layers. Use Hilt for dependency injection, Coroutines and Flow for asynchronous work, Navigation Compose, DataStore for preferences, Room for gallery metadata, MediaStore for media access, Media3 for playback, Coil for thumbnails.
- Camera: CameraX for standard capture and Camera2 interop for advanced manual controls where supported.
- Voice: Android TextToSpeech for guidance. Optional on-device SpeechRecognizer for voice commands. Do not require cloud processing for core accessibility.
- Use scoped storage and MediaStore. Do not request broad storage permissions unless strictly required.
- Provide a working Gradle project with build and run instructions.

CORE PRINCIPLES
1. Accessibility first: TalkBack compatibility, logical focus order, meaningful content descriptions, large touch targets, high contrast, scalable text, haptic and audio feedback, no reliance on color alone.
2. Simple interface: primary actions always visible. Advanced camera and gallery features use progressive disclosure through accessible bottom sheets, dialogs, or expandable panels.
3. Voice guidance: every important state change, control focus, value change, error, and confirmation must be announced. Avoid duplicate announcements with TalkBack.
4. Device capability awareness: detect supported camera features and expose only valid controls. Disabled controls must announce why they are unavailable.
5. Production-grade logic: predictable state machine, lifecycle safety, concurrency control, permission recovery, error handling, and state restoration.
6. Privacy: local-first. No analytics or network use without explicit consent. Location tagging is off by default.

SCREENS AND NAVIGATION
- Welcome screen: app name, short app description, permission explanation, and a large Start button. Announce the screen title and purpose when opened.
- Main screen: hosts a tabbed interface with four tabs: Photo, Video, Gallery, Settings. Use accessible tab labels and announce tab changes.
- Photo tab: camera preview, mode selector, shutter button, flash, timer, zoom, focus, exposure, gallery thumbnail, and access to advanced controls.
- Video tab: camera preview, record and pause controls, recording timer, audio toggle, resolution and frame rate controls, zoom, flash or torch, and access to advanced controls.
- Gallery tab: grid and list views, albums, search, filters, sort, multi-select, item details, edit, share, delete, trash, restore, and slideshow.
- Settings tab: voice guidance, speech rate, verbosity, haptics, theme and contrast, font scale, grid, level, save location, default resolution, timer, geotagging, watermark, and reset options.

CAMERA FEATURE REQUIREMENTS
Support all device-capable features, including:
- Photo, video, portrait, night, panorama, slow motion, time lapse, pro or manual mode, HDR, flash, torch, timer, grid, level, mirror front camera, location tagging, watermark, RAW and JPEG where supported, aspect ratio and resolution selection.
- Zoom, tap to focus, focus lock, exposure compensation, exposure lock, white balance, ISO, shutter speed, focus mode, metering mode, and stabilization where supported.
- Front and back camera switching, wide, ultrawide, and telephoto lenses where supported, burst capture, volume key shutter, orientation lock, review after capture, and countdown announcements.
- Video: resolution, frame rate, bitrate, codec, audio recording, pause and resume, zoom during recording, photo capture during video where supported, and recording timer announcements.
- Every control must have an accessible label, current value, and spoken feedback on change. Unsupported controls must be disabled with a spoken reason.

GALLERY FEATURE REQUIREMENTS
Support all device-capable gallery features, including:
- Browse by date, albums, favorites, screenshots, videos, and recently deleted.
- Grid and list views, thumbnail size, search, filters, sort order, multi-select, select all, share, delete, move to trash, restore, permanently delete with confirmation, and undo where possible.
- Item details: date, time, size, resolution, duration, path, album, and metadata.
- Media viewer: swipe or accessible next and previous controls, play and pause video, seek, zoom, rotate, slideshow, cast where available, and share.
- Editing: crop, rotate, flip, adjust brightness, contrast, saturation, and exposure, apply filters, annotate, draw, add text, trim video, mute video, and export. Destructive edits require confirmation and support undo where feasible.

VOICE GUIDANCE REQUIREMENTS
- Provide a global voice guidance toggle independent of TalkBack.
- Use TextToSpeech with configurable speech rate, pitch, and verbosity.
Guide the user to help align the face very properly. Use google mlkit for detection and position alignment.
- Use a single TTS queue with interrupt rules. Do not interrupt critical error announcements. Debounce rapid value changes such as zoom or exposure.
- Provide optional voice commands through on-device SpeechRecognizer, activated explicitly by the user, not always listening. Support commands such as take photo, start video, stop video, open gallery, next, previous, back, select, delete, confirm, and cancel. Require confirmation for destructive actions.
- Provide haptic and short audio cues for capture, recording start and stop, focus lock, errors, and successful actions.
- Avoid duplicate announcements when TalkBack is active. Use accessibility live regions and announcements appropriately.

ACCESSIBILITY ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
- All interactive elements have accessible names, roles, states, and hints.
- Focus order follows visual and logical order. Focus is restored after dialogs, permission flows, and navigation.
- Touch targets are at least 48 dp. Primary camera controls are larger.
- Text supports system font scaling without clipping. Layout supports large text and landscape.
- Contrast meets WCAG AA. Provide high-contrast theme and color correction support.
- No information is conveyed by color alone. Icons have text labels or content descriptions.
- All controls are operable with TalkBack, Switch Access, and keyboard navigation.
- Camera preview includes an accessible alternative description of the current mode and state. Also announces current scene description in a selected interval in settings if it is on. On device description with mlkit if supported by this or any other alternative.
- Permission rationale is accessible and explains why each permission is needed.


LOGIC AND STATE REQUIREMENTS
- Implement a single source of truth camera state machine: idle, initializing, ready, focusing, capturing, recording, paused, saving, error.
- Prevent concurrent captures. Disable shutter and record controls while saving or transitioning.
- Release camera resources on lifecycle stop and restore state on resume where possible.
- Detect device capabilities at startup and store them in a capability registry. UI derives availability from this registry.
- Handle permission denial, permanent denial, and settings recovery with clear spoken guidance.
- Save media through MediaStore. Use trash and undo for deletions where possible. Confirm permanent deletion.
- Handle orientation changes, low storage, camera in use by another app, and interrupted recordings.
- Use paging and lazy loading for gallery thumbnails. Never block the main thread.
- Provide human-readable and spoken error messages with retry actions.

OUTPUT RULES
- Do not use emojis.
- Keep UI text short, clear, and actionable.
- Prefer standard Android accessibility APIs over custom workarounds.
- Make reasonable assumptions where details are missing and document them.
- Build the app as a coherent working product, not isolated code snippets.
